          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Mantle Volume",
          "value": "84%",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Core Temp",
          "value": "5000-6000°C",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "ixlzrfm"
    },
    {
      "id": "2tphwpr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Sources of Information about Earth's Interior"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Information about Earth's interior comes from direct sources (deep rock drilling, volcanic eruptions) and indirect sources (seismic waves, gravity, magnetic fields).",
        "P-waves (Primary) travel through solids, liquids, and gases, whereas S-waves (Secondary) travel strictly through solid media.",
        "The Crust is categorized into granitic Continental Crust (Sial) and denser basaltic Oceanic Crust (Sima).",
        "The Asthenosphere, located in the upper mantle, is partially molten and serves as the primary source of magma.",
        "The Core is composed primarily of Heavy Nickel and Iron (NIFE), featuring a liquid outer core and a solid inner core."
      ],
      "id": "l2iodkm"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "v27mazy",
      "content": "Direct Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "iw2ty9f",
      "content": "• Deep mining operations."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jzp0rj5",
      "content": "• Scientific drilling projects."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "konr7mn",
      "content": "• Volcanic eruptions which bring material from inside the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "gb838zg",
      "content": "Indirect Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pxq44eb",
      "content": "• Seismic waves generated by earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u8vc1zh",
      "content": "• Study of meteorites."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "07pz68u",
      "content": "• Gravitational and magnetic field studies."
    },
    {
      "id": "ysvulqc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Seismic Waves"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nkhmiwb",
      "content": "• Seismic waves are energy waves generated during earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5zt9yer",
      "content": "• They help scientists understand the structure of Earth's interior."
    },
    {
      "id": "tz6hqjm",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Wave Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Nature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Characteristics</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>P Waves (Primary)</p>",
            "<p>Compressional (body waves)</p>",
            "<p>Travel through solids, liquids and gases; fastest</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>S Waves (Secondary)</p>",
            "<p>Transverse (body waves)</p>",
            "<p>Travel only through solids</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>R Waves (Surface)</p>",
            "<p>Surface waves</p>",
            "<p>Large amplitude and greater destruction</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>L Waves (Surface)</p>",
            "<p>Surface waves</p>",
            "<p>Cause maximum destruction during earthquakes</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Secondary waves or S-waves can travel only through solid materials, which helped scientists understand that the outer core is in a liquid state.",
      "id": "61tw1wf"
    },
    {
